fields.Core Themes JSON:
[
  {
    "title": "Censored Early, Vindicated Late",
    "body": "Chance Garton opens the sixteenth Inner Whirled promising at least monthly episodes, then hands off to Dylan Saccoccio, who explains why the pair avoid current geopolitics after being censored almost a decade ago. Dylan recalls living in Brentwood near Andrew Breitbart, who dropped dead shortly after writing about John Podesta, and argues that the modern system rests on lies invented in the Middle Ages and passed off as ancient history. Citing Eusebius, the father of ecclesiastical history, he maintains that Israel was a Phoenician name for Kronos, time reckoned by the sun, and that the twelve tribes are the twelve signs, matching the twelve apostles and the twelve imams."
  },
  {
    "title": "No Noah Before the Middle Ages",
    "body": "Dylan Saccoccio contends there is no Noah in the record before the Middle Ages, brushing aside the Gilgamesh epic as a text discovered only in the nineteenth century, written in a cuneiform style unmentioned before the seventeenth. He traces the word Semite, meaning a descendant of Noah's son Shem, to Eichhorn in the late 1700s, and notes the British aristocracy's parallel claim to the line of Japheth. Deucalion, he says, surfaces no earlier than the second century AD, while the oldest Greek account of Moses arrives at the end of the third, reversing the usual pattern of Greek mythological imperialism and suggesting Moses was built from the muses."
  },
  {
    "title": "Coins, Arks and Doves",
    "body": "Working from an illustration in a book from the 1600s, Dylan Saccoccio presents a coin of Philip the Arab, emperor from roughly 244 to 249 AD, whose reverse shows four figures, two birds and an ark lettered NOE, with APAMEON below, which the eighteenth century historian Jacob Bryant read as ark. Chance Garton hears apa, father, joined to meon, the unformed being or pure potency. Dylan flags a suspicious IOVA on the obverse, ties Janus and Oannes to a Sicilian coin of the year, and reads Bryant's Analysis of Ancient Mythology on Theban colonies named for the ark, including a people said never to drown."
  },
  {
    "title": "The Mother Named Maris",
    "body": "Exodus 2:5 leaves Pharaoh's daughter unnamed, but Chance Garton points out that the apocryphal book of Jubilees and the writings of Josephus call her Thermuthis, a name he links to the Egyptian cobra and fertility goddess Renenutet and to the Greek therm, heat, joined with metis, wisdom. Better still, Artapanus of Alexandria, an alleged Jewish historian of the second or third century BC known only through Eusebius and Clement, calls her Maris, as does the twelfth century Orthodox patriarch Michael the Syrian. Dylan Saccoccio adds that mar means sea in Latin, making Virgo Marina the virgin of the sea, so Moses, like Jesus, receives a Mary mother."
  },
  {
    "title": "Renaissance Skies, Ancient Claims",
    "body": "Drawing on David Warner Mathisen's Starmyths of the World and the Robert Taylor material behind it, Chance Garton maps Romanelli's Finding of Moses onto the wide part of the Milky Way, casting Sagittarius as Moses's sister, Ophiuchus as Pharaoh's daughter, and Ara or Corona Australis as the basket. He reads the horned face as Aries leading the zodiac, the rejected golden bull as the passage out of Taurus, and the crystal river of Revelation 22:1 as the stream running down to Scorpio. Dylan Saccoccio counters that Ophiuchus was earlier called the toiler and that Fomenko dated Ptolemy's Almagest to the Middle Ages, making the whole tableau modern."
  }
]

